# Geometry restriction grids

Placement on a mesh uses drawing-tool geometry restrictions. A missing cursor means `RestrictionManager.rayTrace` returned nothing.

- `gridSize` is the snap spacing in world units. `radius` is the pick radius. A square cell is only fully covered when the point/line threshold is at least `gridSize / sqrt(2)`. Ignoring `gridSize` leaves gaps between points.
- Point clouds report every point inside that radius. Choose the one closest to the ray, and only among hits near the front of that run. The first hit along the ray is often a neighbor, and on a fine grid that neighbor is rejected as hidden.
- Hideable restrictions are dropped when the scene hits an opaque surface closer than the snap. Compare with a small slack, and also accept a snap that lies on the visible face within the pick radius. Judge that with the hit plane, not depth along the ray: an angled view puts a grid point on that face behind the ray hit. The plane is the first hit that has a face normal. Grid lines and points are often closer, have no normal, and their raycast distance is the foot on the ray. A parallel surface on the hidden side of the plane, such as the boundary mesh 0.05 behind the lines, stays hidden.
- `radius: 0` without `gridSize` must stay a zero threshold. Boundary restrictions use that to avoid picking nearby points.
